{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310675,"nonce":"uBsNyB3IVafoilIFRejCfimouq4=","title":"Probes default to Cranelift; --llvm / RAYZOR_TIER=llvm opts into LLVM tier-3. After this session's optimisations (flash + Q6_K SDOT + ...","message":"After flash attention + Q6_K SDOT + topkScan landed, LLVM and Cranelift\nsit essentially neck-and-neck on Llama 3.2 1B:\n\n| Workload | Cranelift | LLVM | Delta |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| N=64 Paris-history (RUNS=5) | 17.528 tok/s | 17.746 tok/s | +1.2% |\n| MZ 600-tok (single run) | 27.402 tok/s | 26.713 tok/s | -2.5% |\n\nLLVM wins by a hair on short, Cranelift wins by a hair on long. Variance\ndominates — the original \"LLVM is slower\" framing still holds\ndirectionally but the gap is small enough that picking a tier no longer\nmatters for steady-state perf.\n\n**Why**: this session's wins are all in Rust runtime kernels (NEON SDOT,\nfused flash attention, bulk top-K scan). The Haxe-tier compilation only\ncovers the wrapper functions that dispatch into those kernels — Cranelift\ntier-1 already inlines those wrappers down to a single extern call, so\nLLVM tier-3 has essentially nothing left to inline. The tier choice now\nmostly affects startup cost (LLVM compile time during warmup), not\nsteady-state throughput.\n\n\n\n**Rule**: when reporting any perf number, state (1) the backend tier and (2) the run length. Bench results on short runs don't transfer to long-running steady-state.\n\n**The tiered architecture**:\nRayzor runs every program through TieredBackend with a preset config. The lifecycle of any Haxe function:\n1. Starts at the configured starting tier (default 0 = interpreter; `start_interpreted: false` in run_file means start at Cranelift)\n2. Gets promoted as call counts cross thresholds\n3. Reaches the top tier (Cranelift OR LLVM) when blazing_threshold is hit\n\n**Per-preset thresholds (compiler/src/codegen/tiered_backend.rs)**:\n- `Script`: no LLVM\n- `Application` (default): blazing_threshold=2000 → LLVM kicks in after 2000 calls\n- `Server`: blazing_threshold=500 → aggressive LLVM\n- `Benchmark`: blazing_threshold=u64::MAX → manual LLVM upgrade after warmup\n- `Embedded`: interpreter only\n\n**Why short benches show LLVM slower** (validated 2026-06-02):\nFor 24-token llama-chat decode, each Linear.forward is called 7 × 16 = 112 times per token, crossing the 2000 threshold around token 18. The LLVM compile of that function happens MID-DECODE, stealing CPU from token generation. Net: LLVM-tier short runs are slower than Cranelift-only.\n\nMeasured on M1 Pro Llama 3.2 1B Q4_K_M, 7-token decode (EOS):\n- Cranelift only: 7.4s decode, 0.95 tok/s\n- --llvm Application (2000 threshold): 8.4s decode, 0.83 tok/s\n- --llvm Server (500 threshold, even more aggressive): 9.2s decode, 0.76 tok/s\n\nAggressive LLVM promotion = MORE compile work during decode = SLOWER short runs.\n\n**When LLVM tier wins**: long-running workloads where the LLVM compile is amortised over thousands of subsequent calls. Server (long-running inference), batch decode of many sequences, agent loops with hot inner functions called millions of times.\n\n**How probes currently route (post 9719586)**:\n- `cargo test --release -p compiler --lib` — host Rust unit tests, backend-agnostic\n- `./run_haxe_tests.sh` — `rayzor run` per test, default Application preset. Most tests too short for LLVM to fire.\n- `target/release/rayzor run nue/examples/tiny-transformer/Main.hx` — Application preset. Pass `--llvm` to enable LLVM tier-3 promotion threshold.\n- `tools/llama-diff/compare.sh` — Cranelift default. `RAYZOR_TIER=llvm` for LLVM tier (added 9719586).\n- `tools/llama-diff/bench.sh` — same routing as compare.sh.\n- **For honest LLVM bench**: need a long warmup run that lets every hot fn cross blazing_threshold, then measure subsequent runs.\n\n**LLVM build invariant**: Cranelift handles every IrInstruction variant in `compile_instruction`. LLVM must too — when new variants land (MarkMoved, CheckLive most recently), they must be added to both backends. Compile error E0004 (non-exhaustive patterns) is the symptom.\n\n**Investigation hooks for the LLVM perf gap on short runs**:\n1. Use `--preset benchmark` for benchmarks — sets `blazing_threshold = u64::MAX` so LLVM never auto-promotes; emit a manual upgrade signal at the right time\n2. Pre-warm: run the model on a small prompt first, then `rm -rf .rayzor`'s BLADE cache but KEEP the in-process tiered cache, then measure\n3. Or: bench long-form (1000+ generated tokens) so LLVM cost is \u003c 5% of wall time\n4. `compiler/src/codegen/tiered_backend.rs` lines 2482-2555 reference LLVM compile globals + tier-3 promotion — see if eager-promote-all-hot-funcs-after-warmup is exposed as an API\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_probes_cranelift_only` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}